#!/bin/sh
# pre-push -- the wasm32 check, once per push batch.
#
# Why this check and not clippy or the tests: nothing else a developer runs
# locally type-checks `rustdar-web`. Its dependencies are declared under
# [target.'cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")'.dependencies] and its code sits behind
# `#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]`, so a host, Android or iOS build compiles
# the crate as an empty shell and reports success. Only a wasm32 build reads
# the browser code at all. (build.yaml's web-wasm32 row does compile it, by
# `wasm-pack build --release` -- a different workflow, later, and not over
# `--all-targets`, so the crate's tests are not in it.)
#
# And it does not surface cleanly there either. CI runs this same command
# inside the Clippy job, at "Verify the autofixed tree still builds (wasm32)",
# several steps *before* "Run Clippy" (.github/workflows/clippy.yaml). A wasm32
# break therefore aborts that job early, and every lint finding in the tree
# stays hidden behind it until the wasm32 arm compiles again. Catching it here
# is what keeps the lint gate legible.
#
# Once per push rather than per commit: the cost lands on a batch of commits,
# not on each one, and it is the last moment before other people see the work.
#
# `-j 4` because this box is shared: other sessions compile in this checkout at
# the same time, and an unbounded job count starves them.
#
# Refs being pushed arrive on stdin and are deliberately ignored: the tree is
# checked as it stands, whatever is being pushed where.
#
# Inert until opted into with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.

set -u

root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 1
cd "$root" || exit 1

echo "pre-push: cargo check --workspace --all-targets --target wasm32-unknown-unknown"

if cargo check --workspace --all-targets --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -j 4; then
	exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

pre-push: the wasm32 arm does not compile. Pushing this reaches CI as a failed
Clippy job that stops before it lints anything.

If the failure is a missing standard library rather than your code:

    rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown

Host-only test files are a recurring cause -- `--all-targets` compiles the
integration tests for wasm32 too. Gate any host-only test module on
#[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))].

Genuinely need to push first? "git push --no-verify" skips this hook.
MSG
exit 1
